AB  - Video game remakes are designed to recreate the experience of an original work while employing contemporary technology. Understanding how playing originals and remakes enhances the player experience of either version is important for elucidating the value of repeatedly recreating the essence of original works. To investigate this, we compared evaluations from players who had played both versions of various games with those of players who had played only one or other version of those games. Players who had experienced both versions rated the original works significantly higher in terms of their experience in relation to character involvement, real-world simulation, and flaws, and these ratings converged toward their evaluations of the remakes (retrospective effect). This effect was more evident across a broader range of titles than the previously reported forward effect, in which playing an original work influences the experience of the remake. Future research that takes into account the characteristics of individual titles will contribute to a deeper understanding of the mechanisms underlying the retrospective effect. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
