TI  - An integrated space ground cooperative monitoring system for carbon neutral energy hub coordination using multi sensor fusion and edge artificial intelligence
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Engineering Research (Kuwait)
DO  - 10.1016/j.jer.2026.08.008
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047008828?origin=resultslist
AB  - Coordinating economic performance, carbon mitigation, and operational robustness remains challenging in integrated smart energy hubs that couple electricity, gas, heating, cooling, hydrogen, and urban electric mobility under deep uncertainty. Existing approaches commonly rely on static optimization, isolated learning models, or simplified mobility representations, which limits their applicability to traffic-aware and strategically coordinated multi-energy operation. This study develops a focused scheduling framework centered on a trust-region policy optimization controller for low-carbon multi-energy dispatch. An observation layer comprising Internet of Things sensing, Edge AI processing, space–ground meteorological monitoring, and covariance-weighted multi-sensor fusion constructs the state supplied to the controller rather than functioning as independent scheduling modules. Nash and Stackelberg formulations represent strategic interactions among system participants, while information-gap decision theory evaluates the resulting scheduling policy under risk-averse and opportunity-seeking uncertainty modes. The framework was assessed through simulation of an urban benchmark system integrating multiple energy carriers and ultra-light personal electric mobility. Coordinated electricity and carbon trading reduced total operating cost by more than 30% and carbon emissions by over 20% relative to operation without market mechanisms. The trust-region controller achieved the highest training reward, converged approximately 12% faster than distributional reinforcement learning and nearly 30% faster than model-based reinforcement learning, and reduced training time by about 75% compared with the hierarchical approach. Under 20% compounded uncertainty, conventional strategies produced cost deviations exceeding 10%, whereas the proposed scheduling framework maintained the lowest daily operating cost. These findings support the effectiveness of the focused observation–scheduling–coordination architecture for uncertainty-aware operation of mobility-coupled multi-energy hubs. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.
