AB  - Dynamic control for legged robots confronts a fundamental trilemma, where concurrent demands for high agility, substantial payload capacity and energy efficiency impose deeply coupled and often conflicting constraints. We introduce the Agile Hauler Curriculum (AHC), a learning-based method that bypasses complex mathematical modeling to address this problem. The core of AHC is an Elo-based dual-axis dynamic sampling curriculum that continuously focuses training on the agent’s performance frontier, systematically pushing the robot’s agility-payload performance envelope while an energy-aware gating mechanism ensures efficiency. In real-world deployment on the Go2 robot, the AHC-trained policy achieved a max speed of 2.42 m/s with a 12 kg payload, representing a 46.7% increase in speed and a 20.5% average reduction in energy consumption compared to standard grid adaptive curriculum.
